00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:56.630
Let's jump into
breach notification.

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:58.080
What is a breach?

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.460
So when you build systems
on GCP, you build firewalls,

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:06.520
and you have the lowest
right and lowest permissions,

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.074
so people cannot
access the systems.

00:05:08.074 --> 00:05:09.740
But how do you actually
detect a breach?

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.030
How do you know when your
system has been breached?

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.600
So one of the tactics
as well is using

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.720
machine learning to
detect locks, intrusions,

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:19.010
difficult patterns.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:22.390
So one of our use cases,
we're actually using TCP dump,

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:27.230
and it's a very simple tool, to
lock all DNS calls from servers

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:30.470
and as well as all workstations.

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:34.100
And we pipe all these DNS
URL calls into Stackdriver.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.990
And what's great about that
is Stackdriver is serverless.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:40.410
So it can handle as much load
as you give it basically.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:41.960
So it can also
handle this millions

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:46.500
of calls that can occur on a
server or on a workstation.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.060
Now using a recurring
neural network with long,

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.630
short-term memory,
we can detect--

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.670
OK, I'll give you a
background of malware.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.540
When malware attacks one
of your VMs, what it does

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:58.920
is it uses a domain
generation algorithm to try

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.070
to connect to a
common control server

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:02.850
to get further instructions.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.460
To take the next step, you'd
have to destroy information

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:06.870
or to hold it ransom.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.660
So this was one of the tactics
used by the Cryptolocker

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:13.570
ransomware in order to
get further instructions.

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:16.020
So for example, in
your Stackdriver logs,

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:20.150
if you see a domain name in
the bottom left corner there,

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:21.960
it means that your
system has been

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:26.970
attacked by the Cryptolocker
ransomware, and it's infected.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:29.660
The source paper is over here.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:32.710
So what we did was that
we had trained this model

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:34.660
using [INAUDIBLE] Engine.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:38.170
And we provided two
sources of information.

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:42.310
So the first source was using
top 1 million websites that

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:45.850
were real from [INAUDIBLE]
and as well as using

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:48.730
a domain generation algorithms
that were cracked already

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:52.510
to generate large amounts of
training data of malicious DNS

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:53.880
URLs.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.380
And what we do is that we're
training on [INAUDIBLE] engine.

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:59.570
And we also put it in
the cloud data store,

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:03.400
where you detect there
is possible breaches.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:04.810
One thing about
ML models as well

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:07.510
is that you need constant
feedback in order

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:11.170
to continuously
improve your model.

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:14.050
So we also create a web
interface for DevOps guys

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:18.005
to go in and classify
any suspicious errors

00:07:18.005 --> 00:07:20.740
as benign or malicious.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:24.230
This is for probabilities
that are lower than, say, 60%.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.530
So then we can actually
continuously update the model

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:28.240
and improve it.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.700
We also make use of Cloud
Composer, which is basically

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:33.760
Apache Airflow, to continuously
train it once a week

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:38.625
so that it is updated
on-the-fly as we go along.

00:07:38.625 --> 00:07:41.000
I'm going to jump to a quick
demo over here on my screen.

