00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:58.390
So next, the right of access.

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:03.290
What is the right of access?

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:05.630
When a person goes
to your company

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:07.730
and say, give me all
the personal information

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:10.850
you have within
your systems to me,

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:14.340
with GDPR, you have to comply.

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:17.590
And then we talk about
how do you do it.

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:21.060
What do you use the data for.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:23.060
And you actually have to
give all this data back

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.190
in a readable form.

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:27.440
How do you do that when all
your information is stored

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:30.050
in G-Suite and storage
buckets, and you don't have

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:31.940
enough metadata to understand?

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:34.550
How do you do it for pictures,
for maybe your company

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:37.760
photos when you go for outings?

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:40.880
What we built, actually,
we used Google Drive.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:44.690
And we actually scanned through
all the files and Google Docs,

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:47.900
Google sheets, spreadsheets
every time it has been changed,

00:10:47.900 --> 00:10:50.580
as well as PDFs and
image files, first

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:52.940
using Google Vision
APR that also

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:55.380
has OCR capabilities as well.

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.830
So even your credit
card number on a picture

00:10:57.830 --> 00:10:59.390
is considered
personal data as well.

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:05.060
This is triggered using Google
Drive and Google Cloud Storage

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:06.800
push notifications.

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:08.114
They're pushed to Pub/Sub.

00:11:08.114 --> 00:11:10.280
And then what we do is that
we push it to App Engine

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:13.740
as a call, which are called
APIs and try to figure out,

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:16.620
is this personal data or not?

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:20.530
To access this right,
users have to go and supply

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:24.160
their personal data to
search within the system.

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:26.040
So for example,
for me, I submitted

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:29.740
a photo of myself going from
left to right, top to bottom.

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:31.380
And then this is
used as a picture

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:35.340
to scan for all the image
files within Cloud Storage

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:40.094
as well as Google
Drive for me in it.

00:11:40.094 --> 00:11:42.510
So when new images are saved
in the Google Drive and Cloud

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:46.170
Storage, they're actually first
scanned by Google Vision API,

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:47.840
so they can annotate
on the faces.

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:50.330
So we find the coordinates
whether this photo

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:51.940
has a face in it or not.

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:55.210
The reason is that we use
Google Vision API [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:55.210 --> 00:11:58.630
handle method skill compared
to using our open face

00:11:58.630 --> 00:12:00.487
models directly.

00:12:00.487 --> 00:12:02.195
The reason for that
is, like I mentioned,

00:12:02.195 --> 00:12:05.890
it's serverless as
well as much cheaper.

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:08.550
So when we detect there
is a face within an image,

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.680
and this image is not
only stored as JPEG,

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:12.940
but images that are
stored within a Google Doc

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:16.380
or within a Google slide,
they are embedded images

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:18.800
as well we are talking about.

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:21.080
When we detect there is a
face, we extract the face,

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:24.020
find the coordinates, crop
it out, and use OpenFace

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:26.270
to get the vectors of the face.

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:29.570
So your face becomes, basically,
a floating point number vector.

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:32.800
And we store it into BigQuery.

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:34.860
The reason we use
BigQuery is because it

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:37.860
allows us to search for these
images in a very good manner

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:39.334
and very fast manner as well.

00:12:39.334 --> 00:12:41.250
So we search for the
lowest exceeding distance

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:43.577
between, say, my
face and others faces

00:12:43.577 --> 00:12:44.660
stored within the systems.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:50.190
So any measures above
a certain threshold

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.890
are then stored into
Cloud Datastore.

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:57.470
You can actually look at it from
the App Engine website as well.

00:12:57.470 --> 00:12:59.224
I'm just going to
jump into a demo here.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:05.250
You look at the
personal data search.

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:12.915
What I'm going to do is just
submit my face over here,

00:13:12.915 --> 00:13:14.290
something I just
took earlier on.

00:13:18.347 --> 00:13:19.430
So I get a search ID back.

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.590
So what it's doing now
is vectorizing my face.

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:28.708
Give it a few seconds.

00:13:32.145 --> 00:13:33.520
What it's doing
in the background

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:36.145
is actually getting
my face data.

00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:41.490
We actually run up OpenFace
as a container on GKE,

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:44.095
and then getting the
vectors out, and then doing

00:13:44.095 --> 00:13:45.462
a query on BigQuery.

00:13:45.462 --> 00:13:46.380
Let's refresh it.

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:51.490
