00:13:51.490 --> 00:13:55.020
So now, you can see that
I have some images on me

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:57.614
that I put on the
G-Suite system.

00:13:57.614 --> 00:13:59.280
And then you can
actually have the link,

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:03.330
the owner of the images
of the document as well.

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:05.080
Of course, there
are a few others.

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:07.170
That is my son.

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:09.200
It's quite close,
but not close enough.

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:12.896
And some of my colleagues
over here in the bottom.

00:14:12.896 --> 00:14:15.260
So what you do normally,
you do a threshold,

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:17.260
so you can find the closest
match to your faces.

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:20.415
And then you can deliver
it in a document.

00:14:20.415 --> 00:14:22.040
So we can open a
document here as well.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:23.240
[INAUDIBLE] slides.

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:26.640
You see some tests here.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:28.590
And this is how you
look like in BigQuery.

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:51.750
Over here, you can see that
this is how your face looks

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:52.980
like as a vector.

00:14:55.544 --> 00:14:56.960
Can you change
back to the slides?

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:03.200
Thanks.

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:05.010
So now, the right
to be forgotten.

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:07.880
So then we got to build the
next step, which is the right

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:12.010
to be forgotten by a user.

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:14.810
So for example, if a person
answers the question to a quiz

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:17.070
and you store it
using Google Forms,

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:19.860
you store it in a spreadsheet.

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:23.490
After you have entered the
quiz and you got a prize,

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:26.240
you actually can request
to get your data removed

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:30.230
from the system, so that you
can prevent direct marketing.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:32.640
And the user organization
actually have to comply.

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:36.980
Because it is within your rights
to remove that information.

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:40.871
However, the company may
have use for that information

00:15:40.871 --> 00:15:41.370
as well.

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:43.960
You want to keep track of
maybe the answer that you give.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:45.690
So even though your
name identifies you,

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:48.480
maybe the answer that
you gave to the question

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:49.819
does not identify you.

00:15:49.819 --> 00:15:52.110
So that doesn't fall within
the scope of personal data.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:59.300
So when a person submits
a request to be forgotten,

00:15:59.300 --> 00:16:04.290
the DPO will actually go using
the App Engine web interface

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:07.330
to do a request
for access to get

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:10.610
all the hits you get back on
your G-Suite and your buckets.

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:15.800
And what the DPO does
is that it will then

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:17.870
make a few steps to
remove the whole document.

00:16:20.690 --> 00:16:23.952
In G-Suite as well, there's
version handling as well.

00:16:23.952 --> 00:16:25.410
For every single
document you have,

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:27.285
every single spreadsheet,
every single slide,

00:16:27.285 --> 00:16:30.020
there is versioning, which
means that if you just

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:32.450
remove the picture on the
latest version and you save it,

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:33.380
what you can do,
you can actually

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:35.360
go back to the previous
version very easily

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.040
and get back the
pictures again, which

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:40.680
means that you have not really
removed all your personal data

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:41.780
from the system.

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:45.140
And that's something
that's is kind of a catch.

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:49.610
So one possible way is to
remove the whole document if you

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:52.160
don't need the information.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:54.290
Next step is to remove
the relevant information.

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:56.660
Now this is quite a
troublesome procedure.

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:59.480
Because what you need to do is
recreate the entire document,

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:00.890
removing the personal data.

00:17:00.890 --> 00:17:02.224
So now, we have automated that.

00:17:02.224 --> 00:17:04.099
So it actually recreates
the entire document,

00:17:04.099 --> 00:17:05.599
but without the
personal information

00:17:05.599 --> 00:17:09.648
that the user has requested
for to be removed.

00:17:09.648 --> 00:17:13.270
Or you can replace the
information with dummy data.

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:15.739
Or you maybe remove the
sentence or the row.

00:17:15.739 --> 00:17:17.780
So there's a few calls,
a decision-making process

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:21.920
here that the DPO has
to do to actually take.

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:25.609
Now what we use for
training and model is

00:17:25.609 --> 00:17:30.900
we want to imitate the DPO and
try to see what kind of factors

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:34.896
does he take into consideration
to execute the next steps.

00:17:34.896 --> 00:17:37.270
So we look at features such
as number of words in a file.

00:17:37.270 --> 00:17:40.370
If it's a large file, you cannot
remove the whole entire file.

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:42.620
Because you may need certain
information from the file

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:44.540
as well.

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:46.360
So that is a consideration.

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:47.610
When it was last modified?

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:51.640
If it's a very old file, maybe
you don't need it anymore.

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:54.160
The type of personal
information-- is it a picture?

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:55.660
Is your face in the picture?

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:58.030
Maybe it doesn't make sense
to put a right triangle

00:17:58.030 --> 00:17:59.666
in a picture and
reupload it again,

00:17:59.666 --> 00:18:00.790
covering one person's face.

00:18:00.790 --> 00:18:04.010
Maybe just remove the
entire picture directly.

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:06.674
The access rights of the file--
who has access to these files?

00:18:06.674 --> 00:18:09.090
So it's only one person that
has already left the company,

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:12.632
then you can just
remove the document.

00:18:12.632 --> 00:18:14.340
The location of the
file-- so what we did

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:18.230
was restructure the
entire G-suite layout

00:18:18.230 --> 00:18:22.980
as well, so that the
location of the file

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:25.680
determines and
dictates where you

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:28.300
keep different kinds of
personal information.

00:18:28.300 --> 00:18:30.770
So the DPO will make a
decision and then that

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:32.712
is trained to this ML model.

00:18:32.712 --> 00:18:34.670
And this ML model is a
very, very simple model.

00:18:34.670 --> 00:18:38.772
It's just a linear
classifier to give you

00:18:38.772 --> 00:18:40.230
whether it should
be removed, or it

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:43.609
should be redacted, or
replaced with dummy data.

00:18:43.609 --> 00:18:45.900
So what happens is that the
model will make suggestions

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:47.985
through the web interface.

00:18:47.985 --> 00:18:50.506
And then life becomes
easier for DPO.

00:18:50.506 --> 00:18:52.880
And right now, we don't have
enough data to actually make

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:54.720
this decision automatically.

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:57.770
So what we're doing now is
just running a few scenarios

00:18:57.770 --> 00:19:00.500
to generate train data,
so that we can eventually

00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:01.860
automate the system.

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:04.700
So when it comes to invoking
the right to be forgotten,

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:07.010
you can just invoke it,
and we can make a decision.

00:19:07.010 --> 00:19:08.030
Someone has just confirmed it.

00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:09.863
And you can remove all
these documents away.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:13.900
So I'm now going to jump
into privacy by design.

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:18.380
With Google Suite and
Google Cloud Storage,

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:21.120
one of the awesome things
you can do is share files.

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:21.980
You can share files.

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:24.190
You can share by links.

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:26.760
You can add people
in the groups.

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:29.970
But with privacy by
design, what that means to

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:32.561
us is that you have to think of
privacy at the highest level.

00:19:32.561 --> 00:19:35.060
Because when you think about
someone loaning you their data,

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:36.810
that means you're
responsible for it.

00:19:36.810 --> 00:19:38.310
You have to make
sure that you don't

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:40.560
share data
irresponsibly and cause

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:42.520
a problem to your company.

00:19:42.520 --> 00:19:44.970
So any changes in access rights
of file with personal data

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:46.825
should be investigated.

00:19:46.825 --> 00:19:48.450
You mentioned one of
the previous tasks

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:52.350
was identifying any
documents with personal data.

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:53.390
So that is done.

00:19:53.390 --> 00:19:57.000
Now how do you understand which
is a dangerous access right

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:57.870
change?

00:19:57.870 --> 00:20:00.430
How do you do that?

00:20:00.430 --> 00:20:04.300
To get any change in
terms of access changes,

00:20:04.300 --> 00:20:06.570
we use the same driver again.

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:09.910
They have inbuilt
logging of any changes

00:20:09.910 --> 00:20:12.770
to cloud storage
objects, which is great.

00:20:12.770 --> 00:20:15.910
And then this is
published into Pub/Sub.

00:20:15.910 --> 00:20:18.280
It flows so well together
that it just took us

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:19.590
one day to implement this.

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:21.820
It's so fast.

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:24.130
And using Google Drive,
you can subscribe

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:25.670
to push notifications as well.

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:28.070
So every time there's
a permission change,

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:33.470
it also updates you and
tell you, OK, this document

00:20:33.470 --> 00:20:37.170
has been shared with somebody.

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:40.420
So again, we pipe it through
Pub/Sub into App Engine.

00:20:40.420 --> 00:20:42.990
And we also train the model.

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:44.970
And the model detects
whether the access right

00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:47.070
change is correct or not.

00:20:47.070 --> 00:20:48.600
In this case, it's binary only.

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:53.540
Some of the features
that we use,

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:56.440
we use who do you
share the files to.

00:20:56.440 --> 00:20:59.300
For example, in GCS,
you can share objects

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:04.890
into a service account, or you
can share to a group of users.

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:07.190
So if you're sharing
to a group of users,

00:21:07.190 --> 00:21:08.670
it means that you can do that.

00:21:08.670 --> 00:21:11.900
But is that an access
right violation?

00:21:11.900 --> 00:21:14.030
If you share personal
data to a group,

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:15.719
you better do it carefully.

00:21:15.719 --> 00:21:17.510
The location of the
file, like I mentioned,

00:21:17.510 --> 00:21:20.870
we restructured
our file storage,

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:24.782
so that we can put personal
data in the correct places.

00:21:24.782 --> 00:21:26.490
The job title and
department of the user,

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:29.880
we found that quite
useful as well.

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:32.100
For example, the
marketing department

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:36.770
doesn't have to have access to
the CVs that HR have, right?

00:21:36.770 --> 00:21:39.170
But the manager of
marketing department

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:41.790
may need access to the CVs.

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:44.390
So we found that's quite
interesting feature

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:47.060
that we use as well, as well
as the current permissions

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:48.790
the user has.

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:52.800
So with that, we just train
a linear regression model.

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:57.220
And it comes back and tags any
changes in the access rights

00:21:57.220 --> 00:22:02.560
as possibly correct
or incorrect.

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:05.160
DevOps can go in and monitor
these access right changes

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:06.160
as well.

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:09.810
And if you see a large amount
of access changes being made,

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:15.252
it can also detect that and flag
that as a possible violation.

00:22:15.252 --> 00:22:16.710
The last thing I
want to talk about

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:18.570
is the data protection officer.

00:22:21.810 --> 00:22:24.880
GCP is a data processor.

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:27.760
The company using the GCP
is the data controller,

00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:31.080
which, in this case, is
us, Avalon Solutions.

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:32.730
What everything a
company need to do,

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:36.480
you need to designate a
data protection officer that

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:39.450
basically handles all
issues with personal data

00:22:39.450 --> 00:22:42.620
and the protection
of personal data.

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:45.830
So this means that the DPO
can be very, very busy.

00:22:45.830 --> 00:22:48.680
They have to train their
users as well where

00:22:48.680 --> 00:22:50.120
to store the personal data.

00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:51.950
I mentioned there is
locations of where

00:22:51.950 --> 00:22:55.210
you should store your
personal data, in which file,

00:22:55.210 --> 00:22:56.774
in which directory.

00:22:56.774 --> 00:22:58.190
And also, to answer
some questions

00:22:58.190 --> 00:23:02.030
on GDPR and some
company-specific questions.

00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:04.640
So for this, we use Dialogflow
to build a chat bot,

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:09.460
so that our employees can go
in and ask questions about GDPR

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:12.180
and whether is this
personal data or not.

00:23:12.180 --> 00:23:15.170
So a few examples we
got is, for example, we

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:19.280
run Kubernetes workshops.

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:21.740
You can go into the
DPO chat bot and ask,

00:23:21.740 --> 00:23:24.350
where should I save the sign
up list, the spreadsheet?

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:26.840
Even though you
train your people

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:32.480
about where to save personal
data, you can also, on the fly,

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:33.887
ask the DPO chat bot.

00:23:33.887 --> 00:23:35.720
Say, you should save
it into this directory.

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:39.890
You should only share it to
this person and this group,

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:42.830
so that you don't
accidentally leak out

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:45.710
your usernames or
your email addresses.

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:48.350
Another question we often
get from our employees

00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:51.420
is, is a username
considered personal data?

00:23:51.420 --> 00:23:55.220
And then we also have many
discussions about that.

00:23:55.220 --> 00:23:58.640
So we can answer to you as well
that pseudo-anonymous data is

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:02.300
actually considered
personal data as well.

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:04.730
Because it can be linked
back from a Google search.

00:24:04.730 --> 00:24:08.029
Because a lot of people
use their usernames

00:24:08.029 --> 00:24:09.320
in many of different platforms.

00:24:09.320 --> 00:24:13.100
So we could trace back to, say,
Facebook or any social media

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:15.540
profiles and leaking
their true identity.

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:18.420
So this is considered
personal data as well.

00:24:23.230 --> 00:24:26.290
In conclusion, what
I'm trying to show

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:32.420
you is that the GDPR things
that we did, when we use

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:35.900
AI to solve it, it
was kind of like you

00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:38.766
think about it, who wants to
[INAUDIBLE] compliance right.

00:24:38.766 --> 00:24:41.030
It's kind of a dry topic.

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:44.030
But we have found a way to use
some of the latest technologies

00:24:44.030 --> 00:24:47.396
in Google Cloud and in the
world to spice things up,

00:24:47.396 --> 00:24:48.770
so we can learn
things on the fly

00:24:48.770 --> 00:24:51.440
as well, learn machine learning
as well as we go along,

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:54.282
and to test whether
does it actually work.

00:24:54.282 --> 00:24:56.240
And the best way to
understand whether it works

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:58.040
is by using it.

00:24:58.040 --> 00:24:59.660
So we see that it does benefit.

00:24:59.660 --> 00:25:02.210
We do save a lot of time
classifying this information

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:06.420
and tracking this potentially
harmful violations.

00:25:06.420 --> 00:25:09.110
And so that we can go out
and build other cool stuff.

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:13.620
That's all I have.

00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:14.790
My email address is here--

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:16.960
jason.quek@avalonsolutions.com.

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:19.436
So if you have any questions,
feel free to drop me a email.

00:25:19.436 --> 00:25:21.060
And we can discuss
a bit more about how

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:23.610
we built some of these
solutions and things you have

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:27.230
to think about to make sure that
your organization is protected

00:25:27.230 --> 00:25:28.300
in GDPR.

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:28.800
Thanks.

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:31.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

